WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.260
How can genomes date splits that left no fossil, and spot selection?

2
00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:11.020
Our species arose in Africa, yet people with ancestry outside Africa carry a little

3
00:00:11.020 --> 00:00:17.400
Neanderthal DNA. Genomes record history in variants, the sites where they differ.

4
00:00:18.533 --> 00:00:23.533
Start with neutral changes, variants with no effect on survival or reproduction.

5
00:00:24.933 --> 00:00:29.433
On average, they become substitutions at the rate they arise, whatever the

6
00:00:29.433 --> 00:00:35.433
population size. So the substitution rate, K, equals the mutation rate, mu.

7
00:00:37.193 --> 00:00:41.573
Neutral differences pile up steadily, and one dated split turns their count into

8
00:00:41.573 --> 00:00:42.573
years.

9
00:00:43.033 --> 00:00:47.213
Over long spans, a site can change twice, hiding the first change.

10
00:00:48.473 --> 00:00:54.493
So observed difference p is corrected into distance D. For small p the correction is

11
00:00:54.493 --> 00:00:59.553
tiny. At p of 0.30, D is 0.383.

12
00:01:01.153 --> 00:01:04.973
D ÷ p is about 1.28: over a quarter more change.

13
00:01:06.117 --> 00:01:12.117
Departures from this neutral baseline are clues to selection, or to gene flow, genes

14
00:01:12.117 --> 00:01:14.217
moving between populations by mating.

15
00:01:15.617 --> 00:01:20.757
Ancient DNA, from bones tens of thousands of years old, is compared with living

16
00:01:20.757 --> 00:01:27.397
genomes. Place four genomes on one tree: African, Eurasian, Neanderthal, and a

17
00:01:27.397 --> 00:01:28.457
chimpanzee outgroup.

18
00:01:28.457 --> 00:01:32.917
The outgroup marks each site's older version, allele A.

19
00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:40.040
Call a newer, derived variant B, and check sites where the Neanderthal carries B. On

20
00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:44.120
this tree, the African and Eurasian are each other's closest relatives.

21
00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:50.140
Without later gene flow from Neanderthals, which living genome should share B more

22
00:01:50.140 --> 00:01:51.140
often?

23
00:01:56.083 --> 00:01:58.403
Without gene flow, neither should win.

24
00:01:59.643 --> 00:02:03.163
Both reach the Neanderthal only through the same shared ancestor.

25
00:02:04.423 --> 00:02:08.963
Yet people with ancestry in Europe or Asia carry a little Neanderthal DNA.

26
00:02:10.543 --> 00:02:11.983
That is past gene flow.

27
00:02:13.623 --> 00:02:14.623
Patterns can mislead, though.

28
00:02:15.617 --> 00:02:20.297
The clock requires one steady rate across the dated split and the target.

29
00:02:21.617 --> 00:02:24.357
Real substitutions arrive irregularly.

30
00:02:25.617 --> 00:02:30.197
Slightly harmful mutations break the clock, because their rate depends on population

31
00:02:30.197 --> 00:02:35.237
size. Even the sequence tree is an inference with stated support.

32
00:02:36.377 --> 00:02:40.457
So a departure from neutral counts is a clue to selection, not proof.

33
00:02:41.550 --> 00:02:47.990
Until the early 1990s, many researchers favored multiregional evolution: regional

34
00:02:47.990 --> 00:02:50.470
populations each becoming our species.

35
00:02:52.050 --> 00:02:56.150
Living and ancient genomes now strongly support an African origin.

36
00:02:57.550 --> 00:03:01.250
Living people trace most ancestry to African populations.

37
00:03:02.530 --> 00:03:06.950
And people with ancestors outside Africa carry a little Neanderthal DNA.

38
00:03:08.433 --> 00:03:12.173
Proof of selection needs other evidence, like field records.

39
00:03:13.573 --> 00:03:20.933
Boag and Grant, in 1981, compared survivors of the 1977 drought on Daphne Major with

40
00:03:20.933 --> 00:03:22.693
the banded population beforehand.

41
00:03:24.293 --> 00:03:28.093
Larger birds, especially males with larger beaks, survived best.

42
00:03:29.513 --> 00:03:33.833
Beak and body size go together, so survival alone cannot name the target.

43
00:03:33.833 --> 00:03:38.473
Neutral baselines turn departures into testable clues.
